1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,000
There is a number that gets repeated so often in casual conversation about ancient humans that it has become a kind of trivia reflex. Life expectancy in the

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:16,000
stone age was around 30 years old. This number is technically true and almost completely misleading at the same time.

3
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:22,000
And untangling why is one of the more satisfying exercises in historical myth busting that the field of paleo

4
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:30,000
demography has to offer. Because the popular image this number conjures a 30-year-old hunter gatherer collapsing

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:38,000
from old age, creaky joints, gray hair, the whole package, while everyone around them treats this as an entirely

6
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:46,000
unremarkable and expected event is almost completely wrong. And the actual explanation for why that number is so

7
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:55,000
low is both more interesting and considerably darker than simple old-fashioned aging. So let us actually take this number apart and see what is

8
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:03,000
really inside it. The first thing to understand is the difference between life expectancy at birth and life expectancy at a later age. Because these

9
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:10,000
are two completely different statistics that get casually conflated in ways that produce enormous confusion. Life

10
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:19,000
expectancy at birth is an average. It takes every single person born into a population, including those who die within their first hour of life, and

11
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:28,000
averages their age at death across the entire group. This average is extraordinarily sensitive to infant and child mortality. Because death in the

12
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:36,000
earliest years of life pulls the average down dramatically, even if a substantial portion of the population that survives childhood goes on to live a relatively

13
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:43,000
long life by any standard. Here is a simple thought experiment that illustrates exactly why this matters.

14
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:50,000
Imagine a population of 10 people. Five of them die before their fth birthday. A grim but historically realistic infant

15
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:58,000
and child mortality rate for many ancient and even relatively recent preodern populations. The other five survive childhood and live to the ripe

16
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:04,000
old age of 70. What is the average life expectancy at birth for this population?

17
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:14,000
You add up all 10 ages at death. five deaths at roughly age two on average and five deaths at age 70 and divide by 10.

18
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:21,000
The math works out to a life expectancy at birth of roughly 36 years. But notice what this number completely fails to

19
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:31,000
communicate. Half the population lived to 70. Nobody in this population died of old age at 36. The number 36 describes a

20
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:40,000
statistical average that corresponds to the actual lived experience of essentially nobody in the group. This is in simplified form almost exactly what

21
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:47,000
is happening with the famous stone age life expectancy figures that circulate so widely. The low average is driven

22
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:55,000
overwhelmingly by extremely high infant and child mortality, not by widespread early death among adults who survived

23
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:02,000
their childhood. The more revealing and more accurate statistic is life expectancy at a later age, say life

24
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:10,000
expectancy at 15, which measures how much longer an individual could expect to live once they had survived the brutally dangerous early years of

25
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:17,000
childhood. And when pale demographers calculate this statistic for ancient and prehistoric populations, the picture

26
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:26,000
changes dramatically. Studies of huntergatherer populations, both archaeological and from ethnographic observation of contemporary traditional

27
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:33,000
societies, consistently find that individuals who survived to age 15, had a reasonable expectation of living into

28
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:41,000
their 60s or even their 70s. This is not a small correction. This is the difference between a population where everyone dies young and a population

29
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:49,000
where most adults live a genuinely long life by any reasonable historical standard. once you account for the brutal filtering effect of childhood

30
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:58,000
mortality. So why was childhood mortality so catastrophically high? This is where the actual interesting and often genuinely dark substance of this

31
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:06,000
question lives. Infectious disease is the single largest category and the specific pattern of infectious disease risk changed dramatically across

32
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:14,000
different phases of human history in ways that complicate the simple narrative that things just kept getting worse the further back you go. Hunter

33
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:23,000
gatherer populations living in small, mobile, lowdensity groups were actually relatively protected from many of the infectious diseases that became major

34
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:31,000
killers later in human history. Many of the most devastating epidemic diseases, smallox, measles, tuberculosis in its

35
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:39,000
epidemic form, bubonic plague require population densities and patterns of sustained close contact that simply did

36
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:47,000
not exist in small mobile hunter gatherer bands. These diseases are in technical epidemiological terms density

37
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:55,000
dependent. They need a sufficiently large and sufficiently concentrated population to sustain ongoing transmission because if the disease

38
00:04:55,000 --> 00:05:04,000
burns through a small isolated group too quickly, it runs out of new hosts to infect and the chain of transmission collapses. This means that

39
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:12,000
counterintuitively the agricultural revolution and the rise of settled dense population centers actually increased

40
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:20,000
infectious disease mortality substantially compared to the hunter gatherer baseline even as it solved certain other problems related to food

41
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:27,000
security. Agricultural villages and later cities created exactly the conditions. population density,

42
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:35,000
sustained close contact, proximity to domesticated animals that served as reservoirs for zonotic disease, and [snorts] contaminated water supplies

43
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:44,000
from concentrated human and animal waste that allowed epidemic diseases to flourish in ways they simply could not in the dispersed mobile hunter gatherer

44
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:51,000
world. Child birth represents the second major category of mortality risk and its effects were felt across two distinct

45
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:59,000
populations simultaneously, mothers and infants. Maternal mortality in preodern populations was staggering

46
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:08,000
by any contemporary standard. Estimates for various historical and traditional populations place the risk of a woman dying as a direct result of childbirth

47
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:16,000
complications somewhere in the range of 1 to 2% per birth. which sounds modest until you account for the fact that women in these populations typically

48
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:25,000
gave birth many times over the course of their reproductive years. A woman who gave birth eight times across her lifetime with even a conservative 1%

49
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:32,000
mortality risk per birth faced a cumulative lifetime risk of dying in childirth that could easily exceed 5 to

50
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:40,000
8%. meaning that something like 1 in 15 to 1 in 12 women in some historical populations could expect to eventually

51
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:47,000
die as a direct consequence of pregnancy and delivery. The specific causes of maternal death in preodern contexts were

52
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:54,000
grimly consistent across cultures and time periods. Hemorrhage during or immediately after delivery. Infection

53
00:06:54,000 --> 00:07:03,000
following delivery. Obstructed labor in cases where the infant's position or size created a mechanical blockage that no amount of effort or skill could

54
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:11,000
resolve without modern surgical intervention. And pre-clampsia and related hypertensive disorders of pregnancy that remain dangerous even

55
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:20,000
with modern medical care and were essentially untreatable in any preodern context. Infant and child mortality, meanwhile, operated through an even

56
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:29,000
broader range of mechanisms, and the sheer scale of it is genuinely difficult to internalize from a modern perspective. Estimates for a various

57
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:36,000
preodern populations, both historical and prehistoric, suggest that something in the range of 30 to 50% of children

58
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:45,000
died before reaching adulthood, with the most dangerous period overwhelmingly concentrated in the first 1 to 2 years of life. Birth complications,

59
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:53,000
infectious disease in a population with an immature immune system and no access to antibiotics or vaccines, malnutrition

60
00:07:53,000 --> 00:08:00,000
during periods of food scarcity that hit children disproportionately hard. And the simple physical fragility of very

61
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:10,000
young bodies in a world without modern medical intervention combined to produce mortality rates that are almost unimaginable by contemporary standards.

62
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:19,000
This staggering childhood mortality rate is the single largest factor dragging down the famous life expectancy at birth statistics that get casually quoted as

63
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:28,000
evidence that ancient people simply aged faster and died younger across the board. They did not in the sense that matters most for understanding daily

64
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:36,000
lived experience. The children dying in catastrophic numbers were not living shortened adult lives. They were not

65
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:44,000
living adult lives at all. Violence is the third major category and its contribution to ancient mortality has been the subject of considerable

66
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:53,000
academic debate over the past several decades. Some researchers drawing on archaeological evidence of trauma in skeletal remains and on ethnographic

67
00:08:53,000 --> 00:09:00,000
observation of contemporary hunter gatherer and tribal societies have argued that interpersonal violence,

68
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:09,000
including warfare between groups, accounted for a remarkably high proportion of adult male deaths in many prehistoric and traditional societies.

69
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:17,000
Estimates in some specific populations range as high as 10 to 20% of all adult male deaths attributable to violence.

70
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:26,000
Other researchers have pushed back against these high estimates, arguing that the specific archaeological sites used to generate them may not be representative of huntergatherer

71
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:34,000
society. More broadly, accidents and environmental hazards constitute a fourth significant category encompassing

72
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:41,000
everything from animal attacks and falls to drowning and exposure to extreme temperatures. A broken bone that might

73
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:49,000
require a brief hospital visit and a cast in the modern world could in a preodern context lead to infection,

74
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:56,000
prolonged disability, and in severe cases death from complications that modern medicine would treat as routine.

75
00:09:56,000 --> 00:10:05,000
This last point deserves particular emphasis because it is one of the most underappreciated factors in understanding why ancient and preodern

76
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:14,000
mortality rates were so much higher than modern rates across essentially every category of risk. The absence of antibiotics.

77
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:21,000
It is genuinely difficult to overstate how completely antibiotics transformed the relationship between minor injury or

78
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:29,000
infection and death. Before antibiotics, a relatively minor wound that became infected could progress to sepsis and

79
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:36,000
death within days. A process that is now almost entirely preventable with a basic course of inexpensive medication. Child

80
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:44,000
birth infections, surgical infections, pneumonia following a common cold or flu. All of these represented genuine

81
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:53,000
mortal threats throughout almost the entirety of human history, right up until the widespread availability of antibiotics in the midentth century. The

82
00:10:53,000 --> 00:11:00,000
gap between ancient and modern mortality is not primarily a story about ancient bodies being fundamentally weaker or

83
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:08,000
less robust than modern bodies. It is overwhelmingly a story about the specific tools and interventions available to address threats that have

84
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:16,000
always existed. Now with all of this context established, let us return to the central question. What did actual

85
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:22,000
adult aging look like for the substantial portion of the population that survived past childhood?

86
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:27,000
The archaeological and ethnographic evidence here is genuinely fascinating.

87
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:38,000
Skeletal remains from individuals who survived to advanced age in prehistoric populations show clear evidence of the same age- related degenerative changes that affect modern elderly populations.

88
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:48,000
Osteoarthritis, dental wear and loss, evidence of healed fractures, and in some cases evidence of conditions like osteoporosis.

89
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:55,000
Contemporary huntergatherer societies studied extensively by anthropologists such as the Hadza of Tanzania and

90
00:11:55,000 --> 00:12:03,000
various groups in the Amazon basin consistently include elderly individuals well into their 70s and occasionally

91
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:10,000
their 80s. These elderly individuals are not marginal curiosities within their societies. They occupy genuinely

92
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:18,000
important social roles, particularly as repositories of accumulated practical knowledge, as caregivers for grandchildren in what anthropologists

93
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:26,000
call the grandmother hypothesis of human longevity evolution, and as sources of social and political authority within

94
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:34,000
their communities. The grandmother hypothesis deserves specific mention because it represents one of the more compelling evolutionary explanations for

95
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:42,000
why humans, almost uniquely among mammals, evolved to live substantially beyond their reproductive years. The

96
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:49,000
hypothesis argues that post-reproductive women who survived into old age provided substantial fitness benefits to their descendants by assisting with child

97
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:53,000
care, food provisioning, and the transmission of accumulated knowledge.

98
00:12:53,000 --> 00:13:01,000
and that this benefit was significant enough to drive the evolution of an extended post-reproductive lifespan in human females specifically. A pattern

99
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:09,000
that strongly suggests our species has been producing substantial numbers of long lived elderly individuals across a very long stretch of evolutionary

100
00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:17,000
history. If ancient humans genuinely and uniformly died at 30, there would be no evolutionary pressure to develop the

101
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:24,000
kind of extended post-reroductive lifespan that the grandmother hypothesis describes because evolution does not preserve traits that never get

102
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:31,000
expressed. The very existence of a substantial population of long-ived grandmothers is itself powerful evidence

103
00:13:31,000 --> 00:13:38,000
that long adult lifespans were a real and recurring feature of human existence throughout deep prehistory.

104
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:48,000
There is another dimension to this story worth unpacking because it concerns how paleodmographers actually reconstruct age at death data from skeletal remains.

105
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:55,000
Determining the age at which an ancient individual died relies on biological markers including the fusion of growth

106
00:13:55,000 --> 00:14:04,000
plates, changes in the pelvis, dental wear, and microscopic bone density changes. The problem is that these aging

107
00:14:04,000 --> 00:14:11,000
techniques become progressively less precise the older an individual was at death. Distinguishing a 45-year-old

108
00:14:11,000 --> 00:14:18,000
skeleton from a 65-year-old skeleton is genuinely difficult. And for decades, the standard practice involved lumping

109
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:26,000
all adults above roughly age 50 into a single broad old adult category without further discrimination. This

110
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:34,000
methodological limitation had a distorting effect on calculated life expectancy figures. If a population's skeletal sample includes individuals who

111
00:14:34,000 --> 00:14:42,000
actually died in their 60s,7s or 80s, but the analytical method could only confidently place them in a broad 50

112
00:14:42,000 --> 00:14:51,000
plus category, then any life expectancy calculation built on that data systematically underestimates the true extent of long adult lifespans,

113
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:59,000
compressing what was likely a much wider range of older adult ages into an artificially narrow category. It is worth pausing here to look more closely

114
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:08,000
at the specific demographic data behind one of the most frequently cited ancient populations in this entire discussion because the numbers themselves tell a

115
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:16,000
more textured story than any single summary statistic can capture. The skeletal collection from the site of Libbon, an Ohio Native American

116
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:24,000
population dating to roughly 800 to,00 CE and extensively studied by physical anthropologists over several decades,

117
00:15:24,000 --> 00:15:33,000
has become something of a textbook example in paleo demographic research precisely because of how thoroughly it has been analyzed. Initial analyses of

118
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:42,000
the lib and skeletal population produced life expectancy figures that fit neatly into the popular narrative and apparently very low average lifespan

119
00:15:42,000 --> 00:15:47,000
with few individuals appearing to survive much past their 40s or 50s.

120
00:15:47,000 --> 00:15:55,000
Later reanalysis of the same skeletal material using updated and more sophisticated aging methodologies that better accounted for the underestimation

121
00:15:55,000 --> 00:16:01,000
problems already discussed produced a substantially revised picture.

122
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:09,000
A meaningful proportion of the population originally classified into a vague older adult category turned out on more careful examination to include

123
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:17,000
individuals who had lived considerably longer than the original analysis suggested. This case study has become something of a cautionary tale within

124
00:16:17,000 --> 00:16:26,000
paleo demography itself, illustrating how methodological limitations in aging older skeletons can systematically distort our understanding of how long

125
00:16:26,000 --> 00:16:33,000
ancient people actually lived. This pattern of revision is not unique to Liban. Across multiple skeletal

126
00:16:33,000 --> 00:16:40,000
populations studied since the midentth century, a recurring trend has emerged in which initial demographic analyses

127
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:47,000
tend to underestimate the number of genuinely elderly individuals and subsequent reanalysis with improved

128
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:54,000
methods tends to reveal a somewhat older and longer lived population than originally believed.

129
00:16:54,000 --> 00:17:01,000
Let us turn now to comparative studies of contemporary hunter gatherer populations conducted by researchers including Michael Gervin and Hillard

130
00:17:01,000 --> 00:17:09,000
Kaplan whose work compiling demographic data across the Hadza, the Ash of Paraguay, the Hiwi of Venezuela and

131
00:17:09,000 --> 00:17:16,000
Colombia and several other groups found a remarkably consistent pattern. High mortality risk concentrated heavily in

132
00:17:16,000 --> 00:17:25,000
the first few years of life. A substantial drop during adolescence and prime reproductive years and then a gradual increase beginning around age 40

133
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:36,000
or 50 that mirrors the mortality curves observed in modern industrialized populations just shifted to higher absolute rates at every age due to the absence of modern medical intervention.

134
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:45,000
Their research produced a specific statistic that deserves wider recognition. Across the various huntergatherer populations studied, the

135
00:17:45,000 --> 00:17:54,000
modal age of adult death, the single most common age at which adults actually died, as opposed to the mean average dragged down by childhood mortality,

136
00:17:54,000 --> 00:18:01,000
clustered consistently in the range of 70 to 72 years old. This is genuinely striking because it suggests that the uh

137
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:08,000
single most statistically common outcome for an adult hunter gatherer who survived early childhood was not dying

138
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:16,000
at 30 or 40 but living into their seventh decade. There is also a worthwhile comparison to be made with the demographic transition that occurred

139
00:18:16,000 --> 00:18:27,000
during the early agricultural period because evidence increasingly suggests the shift from foraging to farming was not demographically speaking a straightforward improvement.

140
00:18:27,000 --> 00:18:34,000
Skeletal evidence from early agricultural populations consistently shows reduced average stature, increased

141
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:41,000
markers of skeletal stress, higher rates of dental disease, and in some populations, evidence of increased

142
00:18:41,000 --> 00:18:50,000
rather than decreased childhood mortality during the initial transition to settled agricultural life. This is sometimes called the Neolithic mortality

143
00:18:50,000 --> 00:18:59,000
crisis and it complicates the simple linear narrative in which human health steadily improved as civilization advanced. There is also a specific and

144
00:18:59,000 --> 00:19:07,000
somewhat counterintuitive point worth raising about how disease environment shifted across the broad sweep of human history in [snorts] ways that complicate

145
00:19:07,000 --> 00:19:12,000
any simple story of continuous improvement or continuous decline.

146
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:20,000
periods of severe epidemic disease, including the wellocumented waves of bubonic plague that swept through medieval Europe, the catastrophic

147
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:27,000
depopulation of the Americas following European contact, and recurring waves of chalera and typhoid in densely populated

148
00:19:27,000 --> 00:19:35,000
preodern cities, all represent acute crises superimposed on top of already significant background mortality rates.

149
00:19:35,000 --> 00:19:43,000
A hunter gatherer population living in relative isolation faced a meaningfully different disease environment than an agricultural population living in a

150
00:19:43,000 --> 00:19:52,000
crowded medieval city during an active outbreak of plague. And lumping both into a single undifferiated category called ancient humans obscures more than

151
00:19:52,000 --> 00:20:00,000
it reveals. There is one more thread in this story that deserves attention before we close because it concerns how this whole topic intersects with our own

152
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:08,000
modern psychological relationship to aging and the way we tell stories about the past. The persistent popularity of

153
00:20:08,000 --> 00:20:16,000
the 30-year myth serves a kind of narrative function. The idea that ancient humans simply dropped dead at 30

154
00:20:16,000 --> 00:20:25,000
fits neatly into a broader cultural story in which modernity represents straightforward, linear improvement over a brutal and short-lived past,

155
00:20:25,000 --> 00:20:33,000
flattering the present by exaggerating the deficiencies of everything that came before it. The more accurate picture changes how we think about what modern

156
00:20:33,000 --> 00:20:42,000
medicine has actually accomplished. The achievement is not that we have somehow extended the maximum potential human lifespan dramatically beyond what was

157
00:20:42,000 --> 00:20:50,000
previously possible. Since long adult lifespans into the 70s and beyond were always within the range of human biological possibility. The actual

158
00:20:50,000 --> 00:20:59,000
achievement is more specific and in its own way more remarkable. We have dramatically reduced the proportion of any given population that fails to

159
00:20:59,000 --> 00:21:06,000
survive childhood, that dies from infectious disease in adulthood, that dies from childbirth complications, and

160
00:21:06,000 --> 00:21:12,000
that dies from injuries that would once have been fatal, but are now routinely treatable.

161
00:21:12,000 --> 00:21:21,000
We have not extended the ceiling of human lifespan nearly as dramatically as popular imagination suggests. We have instead dramatically raised the floor.

162
00:21:21,000 --> 00:21:30,000
This is both a more accurate and a genuinely more remarkable story than the simple myth it replaces. It is the story of a species that learned gradually and

163
00:21:30,000 --> 00:21:39,000
through immense collective effort across many generations to stop so many of its own members from dying before they ever got the chance to discover how long they

164
00:21:39,000 --> 00:21:47,000
might otherwise have lived. The number 30 captures something real and tragic about ancient human existence. It simply

165
00:21:47,000 --> 00:21:55,000
does not capture what most people assume it captures. And the actual story underneath it is both darker in its specific details about childhood and

166
00:21:55,000 --> 00:21:58,000
childbirth and brighter in its implications about adult longevity than the flattened popular version most people carry around in their heads.
